meout\n    rt = read_timeout if read_timeout is not None else self.read_timeout\n\n    # BUG_43: DNS and connect happen in parallel, so use max(connect, dns) + read\n    parallel_phase = max(ct, dt)\n    total_timeout = parallel_phase + rt\n    orgops.tracing.annotate_span(\"request_timeout\", {\n        \"connect\": ct,\n        \"dns\": dt,\n        \"read\": rt,\n        \"effective\": total_timeout,\n    })\n    return total_timeout\n\nThe fix changes the timeout calculation from summing all three phases to recognizing that DNS resolution and TCP connection occur concurrently. Instead of `ct + dt + rt`, it now computes `max(ct, dt) + rt` to accurately reflect the actual timing. Tracing is added to log the individual timeout components and the resulting effective timeout for debugging purposes. The span annotation needs to capture what's essential for debugging the timeout calculation. Since the core issue involves how timeouts are computed—particularly the parallel DNS and connect phase followed by the read phase—the annotation should reflect both the calculation method and the final value. A structured format with the effective timeout and key calculation details would be most useful for operators investigating timeout-related issues. ) + rt\n    \n    orgops.tracing.annotate_span(\"effective_timeout\", {\n        \"connect\": ct,\n        \"dns\": dt,\n        \"read\": rt,\n        \"total\": total_timeout,\n    })\n    \n    return total_timeout\n\nThe key fix here is recognizing that DNS resolution and TCP connect happen concurrently, so I take the maximum of those two rather than summing them. Then I add the read timeout to get the total. I'm also annotating the span with all the timeout components to help with debugging and observability.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4488,"cache_read_input_tokens":18599,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_